; iter_slice_past_the_end: an ITER whose end index is past the vec raises
; rather than iterating a short slice.
;
; The neighbouring vectors cover the two empty cases -- iter_empty_skip and
; iter_inverted_skip both skip the body without faulting -- so nothing
; distinguished "empty, so skip" from "out of range, so raise". Rust's
; resolve_iter_slice validates the slice before it charges the copy;
; xqvm_py charged first and let the element read raise afterwards, which
; is the same fault under a generous budget and a different one under a
; tight one.
;
; The vec holds two elements and the slice asks for three.

VECI r0
PUSH 1
VECPUSH r0
PUSH 2
VECPUSH r0

PUSH 0           ; start_idx
PUSH 3           ; end_idx -- one past the end
ITER r0
NEXT
HALT
